  "body": "## What changed\n\nThis PR adds the pure tool component model to `camcraft-tools`. It is the first issue of the component track (#3642, then #3643 to #3648). It adds no ECS code, no file I/O and no new crate edge.\n\n- `component.rs`: `ToolComponent` with the three ISO 13399 kinds.\n  - `CuttingItem`: an `InsertSpec`, or a solid `Tool` with its shank connection.\n  - `ToolItem`: one machine side (MCS) and one or more insert seats (`CSW1`, `CSW1_2`, ...).\n  - `AdaptiveItem`: one MCS and one CSW.\n  - `ToolComponent::new` checks the component when it is made. The fields are private, so each component that exists is valid.\n  - The check covers the insert and seat specs that it gets. Their fields are public, so the constructor runs `InsertSpec::check` and `InsertSeatSpec::check`. Each refusal names the component, the seat port and the position.\n  - The constructor stores each connection in its canonical form (`ConnectionCode::canonical`).\n- `connection_code.rs`: `ConnectionCode`, one normalized type for ADINTMS and ADINTWS. It has steep tapers (BT, CAT, SK), HSK, PSC (ISO 26623), KM (ISO 26622), ER (ISO 15488), Weldon (DIN 1835-B), cylindrical shank, TTS, rectangular shank (ISO 5608), VDI, BMT, shell mill arbor and a custom name. Sizes must be in the published series.\n- `insert_codes.rs` and `insert_iso1832.rs`: the ISO 1832 insert code, positions 1 to 7 (shape, clearance, tolerance, fixing, size, thickness, corner). The inscribed circle is derived from the shape.\n- `insert_seat.rs`: `InsertSeatSpec` (clamping, shape, approach angle, clearance, hand, size, optional thickness) and the ISO 5608 holder code parser.\n- `component_fit.rs`: `check_insert_fit`, `check_coupling_fit`, `compatible_parents` and `compatible_children`. Each refusal (`FitError`) gives both component names, the property, the expected value and the actual value.\n- `docs/decisions/3642-tool-components.md` records the rules and what was dropped.\n\n## Validation rule (review round 1)\n\n- `InsertSpec::check` refuses a tolerance letter off the table or in lower case, a size off 01 to 99, a corner that the shape cannot have, a bad suffix character, and a hand that the suffix does not state. Then it confirms that the value is the parse of its own text. This closes the whole class.\n- `InsertSeatSpec::check` refuses an insert size off 01 to 99. The letters, the style and the thickness are types that hold only valid values.\n- `check_insert_fit` runs the two checks again as a guard (`FitProperty::Code`).\n- `ConnectionCode::canonical` gives the value that the text of a code parses to. `takes` compares canonical forms, and an invalid code mates nothing. Thus validation, the text round trip, the component and the fit agree.\n\nThe rules are general machining practice. The code tables are the ones that tool vendors publish. No standard text is copied.\n\n## Readings\n\n- `CNMG120408` gives IC 12.7 mm, thickness 4.76 mm and nose radius 0.8 mm. `TCMT110204` gives IC 6.35 mm: the edge is 10.998 mm, and the size code is the edge cut to whole mm after 0.02 mm.\n- `PCLNR2525M12` gives clamping P, approach angle 95 degrees, a 25 x 25 shank and a length of 150 mm.\n- Example refusal text: `'SPKN1203EDL' does not fit 'CSSPR2525M12': hand - expected R, actual L`.\n\n## Pinning tests (camcraft-tools lib)\n\n- `insert_designation_tests::common_turning_inserts_parse`\n- `insert_designation_tests::common_milling_inserts_parse`\n- `insert_designation_tests::designations_round_trip_through_their_text`\n- `insert_designation_tests::malformed_insert_codes_are_refused_by_position`\n- `insert_designation_tests::turning_holder_codes_parse_and_round_trip`\n- `insert_designation_tests::malformed_holder_codes_are_refused_by_position`\n- `insert_designation_tests::connection_codes_round_trip_and_refuse_by_name`\n- `insert_designation_tests::a_plain_bore_takes_a_weldon_shank_but_not_the_reverse`\n- `insert_designation_tests::degenerate_codes_never_panic` (5000 pseudo-random texts, with empty, short and non-ASCII input)\n- `component_fit_tests::inserts_fit_the_holders_made_for_them`\n- `component_fit_tests::insert_mismatches_are_refused_by_name` (shape, clearance, size, thickness, clamping and fixing, hand, port, kind)\n- `component_fit_tests::couplings_match_by_connection_code` (coupling code, Weldon flat)\n- `component_fit_tests::the_library_queries_find_parents_and_children`\n- `component_fit_tests::malformed_components_are_refused_by_name`\n- `insert_designation_tests::directly_built_inserts_and_seats_are_refused_by_position` (review round 1)\n- `insert_designation_tests::typed_connection_codes_normalize_to_their_text_form` (review round 1)\n- `component_fit_tests::components_check_their_specs_and_normalize_their_connections` (review round 1)\n\n## Gate (Windows, winbuild)\n\nAll runs are on the final head `d36523d9d69e815b1d0d69508feb70c07d50fc54`, on Windows (winbuild, `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=5`).\n\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` (log `gate-4096-d36523d9d.log`, first line is the head SHA): the plan is 1 step, `tools suites: lib`. Result: ok, 165 passed, 0 failed, 0 stalled.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `cargo xtask gate light`: 6 steps ok, 0 failed, 0 stalled.\n- P6: no heavy step. No post or toolpath changed, so no `example_catalog*` suite is needed.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No caller uses the model yet. The assembly resolver (#3643), the document table and commands (#3644), the stores and the project file (#3645), multi-seat cutters (#3646), MCP (#3647) and the UI (#3648) come later.\n- No Mac or Linux build. The code is plain Rust with no platform code.\n\n## Left for #3643 and later\n\n- The components have no serde. #3645 owns the `camcraft-components/1` format.\n- The ids are plain strings. #3644 and #3645 decide how ids are made and kept.\n- The ISO 5608 parser reads rectangular-shank holders. A boring bar (ISO 6261) or a cutter body gets its seats as `InsertSeatSpec` values made directly.\n- A seat has no frame (position or rotation). The frames are #3643.\n\nNo command is added, so no MCP tool is owed.\n\nChangelog: Tool components: CamCraft reads ISO 1832 insert codes and ISO 5608 holder codes, and checks if an insert fits a holder seat and if a shank fits a coupling, with a named reason for each mismatch.\n\nEffort: high\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nFixes #3642\n\n",
